TITLE:: SuperMIDI
summary:: A fast and flexible MIDI mapping utility for SuperCollider
categories:: External Control>MIDI
related:: Classes/MIDIdef, Classes/Pbindef, Classes/Bus

DESCRIPTION::
SuperMIDI provides an automated way to manage MIDI CC mappings. It handles Control Bus creation, value scaling with curves, and soft-takeover logic automatically. It is designed to work with general parameters, Pbindef keys and transport (play / stop / pause).

All mappings are created with auto-generated names, backed by control buses that can be read by any running Synth or Pbindef. Mappings can be saved to and loaded from JSON files.


CLASSMETHODS::

private:: bus, map, midiOut, path, prMakeBus, prMakeData, prMakeID, prMakeMap, prPatternAction, prRebuild, prResolvePath, prScale, prTakeoverCheck, prValidate, printValue

METHOD:: init
Initialize the MIDI client.
ARGUMENT:: midiOutPort
Integer. The index of the MIDI output port to use for sending initial CC values to the controller.

METHOD:: map
Map a specific CC number and channel.

ARGUMENT:: type
Symbol. The controller behaviour. \control, \toggle, \moment (momentary toggle).

ARGUMENT:: target
Symbol. The name of the parameter name or pattern key to control. (e.g., \amp).

ARGUMENT:: cc
Integer 0 – 127. The CC number to listen on.

ARGUMENT:: chan
Integer 0 – 15. The MIDI channel.

ARGUMENT:: min
Number. The minimum output value.

ARGUMENT:: max
Number. The maximum output value.

ARGUMENT:: init
Number. The initial value written to the bus at mapping time, and sent back to the controller as a CC value. Should be within the min–max range.

ARGUMENT:: curve
Symbol or Number. The scaling curve applied to the 0 – 127 MIDI input range.
list::
## code::\lin:: — linear mapping
## code::\exp:: — exponential mapping (note: min must be > 0 for exp; values ≤ 0 are clamped to 0.001 internally)
## code::\sin:: — sinusoidal
## a Number — custom curve via code::lincurve::; 0 = linear, positive values = upward, negative values = downward
::

METHOD:: learn
Uses MIDI Learn to map a parameter. It waits for the first incoming CC message to determine the CC number and channel automatically. Useful when you do not know the CC or channel of a particular control.
After calling learn, the post window shows: code::SuperMIDI: move your parameter or press a toggle::. Move the desired control on your hardware and the mapping is created immediately.

ARGUMENT:: type
Symbol. The controller behaviour. \control, \toggle, \moment (momentary toggle).

ARGUMENT:: target
Symbol. The name of the parameter name or pattern key to control. (e.g., \amp).

ARGUMENT:: min
Number. The minimum output value.

ARGUMENT:: max
Number. The maximum output value.

ARGUMENT:: init
Number. The initial value written to the bus at mapping time, and sent back to the controller as a CC value. Should be within the min–max range.

ARGUMENT:: curve
Symbol or Number. The scaling curve applied to the 0 – 127 MIDI input range.
list::
## code::\lin:: — linear mapping
## code::\exp:: — exponential mapping (note: min must be > 0 for exp; values ≤ 0 are clamped to 0.001 internally)
## code::\sin:: — sinusoidal
## a Number — custom curve via code::lincurve::; 0 = linear, positive values = upward, negative values = downward
::

METHOD:: pmap
Map a specific CC number and channel to a key inside a Pbindef. Identical argument structure to code::.set::, with the addition of code::patternName::.

ARGUMENT:: type
Symbol. The controller behaviour. \control, \toggle, \moment (momentary toggle).

ARGUMENT:: target
Symbol. A Pbindef key such as code::\dur::, code::\degree::, code::\amp::, or one of the transport symbols code::\play::, code::\stop::, code::\pause::.

ARGUMENT:: patternName
Symbol. The name of the Pbindef to target.

ARGUMENT:: cc
Integer 0 – 127. The CC number to listen on.

ARGUMENT:: chan
Integer 0 – 15. The MIDI channel.

ARGUMENT:: min
Number. The minimum output value.

ARGUMENT:: max
Number. The maximum output value.

ARGUMENT:: init
Number. The initial value written to the bus at mapping time, and sent back to the controller as a CC value. Should be within the min–max range.

ARGUMENT:: curve
Symbol or Number. The scaling curve applied to the 0 – 127 MIDI input range.
list::
## code::\lin:: — linear mapping
## code::\exp:: — exponential mapping (note: min must be > 0 for exp; values ≤ 0 are clamped to 0.001 internally)
## code::\sin:: — sinusoidal
## a Number — custom curve via code::lincurve::; 0 = linear, positive values = upward, negative values = downward
::

METHOD:: plearn
Uses MIDI Learn to map a parameter to a key inside a Pbindef. It waits for the first incoming CC message to determine the CC number and channel automatically. Useful when you do not know the CC or channel of a particular control.
After calling learn, the post window shows: code::SuperMIDI: move your parameter or press a toggle::. Move the desired control on your hardware and the mapping is created immediately.

ARGUMENT:: type
Symbol. The controller behaviour. \control, \toggle, \moment (momentary toggle).

ARGUMENT:: target
Symbol. A Pbindef key such as code::\dur::, code::\degree::, code::\amp::, or one of the transport symbols code::\play::, code::\stop::, code::\pause::.

ARGUMENT:: patternName
Symbol. The name of the Pbindef to target.

ARGUMENT:: min
Number. The minimum output value.

ARGUMENT:: max
Number. The maximum output value.

ARGUMENT:: init
Number. The initial value written to the bus at mapping time, and sent back to the controller as a CC value. Should be within the min–max range.

ARGUMENT:: curve
Symbol or Number. The scaling curve applied to the 0 – 127 MIDI input range.
list::
## code::\lin:: — linear mapping
## code::\exp:: — exponential mapping (note: min must be > 0 for exp; values ≤ 0 are clamped to 0.001 internally)
## code::\sin:: — sinusoidal
## a Number — custom curve via code::lincurve::; 0 = linear, positive values = upward, negative values = downward
::

METHOD:: mapList
Print a formatted summary of all current mappings to the post window. Output is sorted alphabetically by mapping name.

ARGUMENT:: keyword
String or nil. When given, only mappings whose auto-generated name contains the keyword are shown. Default: code::nil:: (show all).

DISCUSSION::
code::
SuperMIDI.mapList;           // list everything
SuperMIDI.mapList("freq");   // only mappings related to freq
SuperMIDI.mapList("amp");   // only mappings related to amp
::

METHOD:: busList
Print a formatted list of all active control buses to the post window.

ARGUMENT:: keyword
String or nil. Filters results the same way as code::.mapList::. Default: code::nil:: (show all).

DISCUSSION::
code::
SuperMIDI.busList;
// -> e.g.  control_freq_1_0 | Bus(control, 8, 1, server: localhost)
::

METHOD:: remove
Remove a single mapping by name, freeing its link::Classes/MIDIdef:: and link::Classes/Bus::.

ARGUMENT:: name
Symbol. The mapping name.

DISCUSSION::
code::
// Remove the freq mapping created by SuperMIDI.set(\control, \freq, cc: 1, chan: 0)
SuperMIDI.remove(\control_freq_1_0);
::

METHOD:: removeAll
Remove all mappings and free all buses. Internally calls code::MIDIdef.freeAll:: and disposes every bus in the bus dictionary.

DISCUSSION::
code::
SuperMIDI.removeAll;
// -> "SuperMIDI: all mappings and buses removed"
::

warning::
code::removeAll:: calls code::MIDIdef.freeAll::, which removes strong::ALL:: MIDIdefs, including any you may have created independently of SuperMIDI.
::

METHOD:: save
Saves all current mappings to a JSON file at the resolved path. The file stores every parameter needed to fully recreate all MIDIdefs and buses on a future code::.load::.

ARGUMENT:: savePath
String Optional path to set a custom JSON save path.

DISCUSSION::
code::
SuperMIDI.save;
// -> "SuperMIDI: mappings saved to /path/to/myPatch_SuperMIDI.json"
::

METHOD:: load
Load and apply mappings from a JSON file at the resolved path, recreating all MIDIdefs and buses. Any existing mappings with the same name are overwritten; others are left untouched.

ARGUMENT:: loadPath
String Optional path to load a custom JSON load path.

DISCUSSION::
code::
SuperMIDI.load;
// -> "SuperMIDI: mappings loaded from /path/to/myPatch_SuperMIDI.json"
::

METHOD:: trace
print the name, MIDI value and scaled value of mapped parameter

EXAMPLES::

SUBSECTION:: Basic setup — synth parameter control

code::

s.boot;

(
SynthDef(\tone, { |out = 0, freq = 440, amp = 0.5, cutoff = 2000, gate = 1|
    var filt = RLPF.ar(Saw.ar(freq), cutoff, 0.3);
    var env  = EnvGen.kr(Env.adsr(0.02, 0.1, 0.8, 0.3), gate, doneAction: 2);
    Out.ar(out, (filt * amp * env).dup);
}).add;
)

(
SuperMIDI.map(\control, \freq,   cc: 1,  min: 80,   max: 1200, curve: \exp);
SuperMIDI.map(\control, \cutoff, cc: 2, min: 200,  max: 8000, curve: \exp);
SuperMIDI.map(\control, \amp,    cc: 3,  min: 0,    max: 0.9,  init: 0.5);
)

x = Synth(\tone);

(
x.map(\freq,   SuperMIDI.bus[\control_freq_1_0]);
x.map(\cutoff, SuperMIDI.bus[\control_cutoff_74_0]);
x.map(\amp,    SuperMIDI.bus[\control_amp_7_0]);
)
// Check the current bus value while moving the knob
SuperMIDI.bus[\control_freq_1_0].getSynchronous;
::


